WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:08.900
We're now going to look at some additional considerations for ZOS UNIX.

2
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.860
There are several ways to provide print services to an application.

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:19.340
A ZOS UNIX application can use the POSIX functions printf, fprint, write, or fwrite

4
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.920
to place output in the hierarchical file system.

5
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:28.020
The file can either be specified as standard out or by a file name depending on the function

6
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.620
call used.

7
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:33.760
The output residing in the file system can be printed by a batch job using dd statements

8
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:40.940
referring to the out file, by TSOE print options, or by using the lp shell command.

9
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:46.600
This command dynamically allocates a ZOS sysout data set, opens writes and closes it, and sends

10
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.100
it to the ges pool, which sends it to a printer.

11
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:56.100
Each ZOS UNIX user has a number of default printers specified in different ways.

12
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.460
The system will select a printer in the following order.

13
00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:05.380
So first of all, the printer in the dest option of the lp shell command or the printf or fprintf

14
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:06.460
functions.

15
00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:10.620
The printer specified in the lp dest environment variable is next.

16
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:15.460
And then three, the printer specified in the printer environment variable.

17
00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.620
Finally there's four, which is the printer in the rackf user profile.

18
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.700
Currently HFS files are protected with POSIX permission bits.

19
00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:29.740
HFS permission bits allow the installation to specify read authority, write authority, or

20
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:33.180
search authority for a directory.

21
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.260
They also allow specification of read, write, or execute authority for a file.

22
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:42.820
Because there are three sets of bits, separate authorities can be specified for the owner

23
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:48.020
of the file or the directory, the owning group, and everyone else, such as rackf's

24
00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:52.820
Universal Access Authority or UACC.

25
00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:59.060
The owner is represented by a UID, the owning group is represented by a GID.

26
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:06.340
Access checking compares the user's UID and GID to the one stored in the FSP.

27
00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:13.140
When a security decision is needed, the file system calls rackf and supplies the FSP.

28
00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.340
Rackf makes the decision, does any necessary auditing, and returns control back to the

29
00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:20.100
file system.

30
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:25.660
Rackf does not provide commands to maintain the FSP, but it does provide SAF services

31
00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:29.220
that do the FSP maintenance.

32
00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:34.260
COS Unix provides commands that invoke these SAF services.

33
00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:40.460
Access control lists or ACLs can be used to control access to files and directories

34
00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:45.420
by individual user, UID, and by group, the GID.

35
00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.420
ACLs are used along with permission bits.

36
00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:55.860
ACLs are created, modified, and deleted using the setfacl shell command.

37
00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.980
The HFS and ZFS file systems support ACLs.

38
00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:06.220
The ACLs are created and checked by rackf, not by the kernel or file system.

39
00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.380
There are two kinds of ACL entries.

40
00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:13.580
Base ACL entries are the same as permission bits, so owner, group, and other.

41
00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:17.740
They can be changed using chmod or setfacl.

42
00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:22.900
Extended ACL entries are ACL entries for individual users or groups.

43
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.260
Like the permission bits, they're stored with the file, not in rackf profiles.

44
00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:36.300
Each ACL type, access, file default, and directory default can contain up to 1024 extended ACL entries.

45
00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:42.300
Each extended ACL entry specifies a qualifier to indicate whether the entry pertains to a user or a group,

46
00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:48.580
the actual UID or GID itself, and the permissions being granted or denied by this entry.

47
00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:53.660
The allowable permissions are read, write, and execute.

48
00:03:53.660 --> 00:04:02.740
The commands setfacl and getfacl have been introduced in order to create, modify, and display ACL entries specified by the path,

49
00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.500
and here's the syntax for those.

50
00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:09.980
irrska00 is the file checking algorithm.

51
00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:15.340
This callable service has been updated for ACLs and the new UNIX PRIV profiles.

52
00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:22.500
Permissions bits, ACLs, and various UNIX PRIV profiles are used by rackf while performing file access checks.

53
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:27.300
The rackf basic file checking algorithm checks the owner bits, user ACL entries,

54
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.540
union of group bits, and group ACL entries, and other bits.

55
00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:39.500
Now, it's not always necessary to apply ACLs on every file or directory within a subtree.

56
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:46.900
If you have a requirement to grant access to an entire subtree, for example, a subtree specific to a given application,

57
00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.580
access can be established at the top directory.

58
00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:54.660
If a given user or a group does not have search access to the top directory,

59
00:04:54.660 --> 00:05:03.260
no files within the subtree will be accessible regardless of the permission bit set or ACL contents associated with those files.

60
00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:09.380
The user or the group still needs permission to the files within the directory subtree where appropriate.

61
00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:16.260
If this is already granted by the group or other bits, no ACLs are necessary below the top directory.

62
00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:20.780
In the next video, we'll look at some remaining at ZOS UNIX considerations.
